1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Today I'm in East Longmeadow, Massachusetts,

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
in the Cardamundi factory.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Now, you may not have heard of them,

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
but I can guarantee you heard of what they make right here.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
One of the top games of all time,

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
played by more than a billion people.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
It's a little game from Hasbro that you might know

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
called Monopoly.

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Some of our top toys and games have endured for generations,

10
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:33,000
centuries, even millennia.

11
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:37,000
And the business of play earns some serious money.

12
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:39,000
The global market for toys and games

13
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:42,000
is more than $92 billion a year.

14
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:44,000
And that's not monopoly money.

15
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:46,000
That's the real thing.

16
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:49,000
I want to know how this industry continues

17
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:51,000
to sell our favorite top toys and games

18
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:55,000
while innovating and creating completely new ones every year.

19
00:00:55,001 --> 00:01:02,000
How does Hot Wheels design the 130 new car models introduced every year?

20
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:09,000
And how is Mattel using cutting edge tech to bring He-Man to a new generation of fans?

21
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:13,000
The spinners landed on me and it is my turn to find out. Game on!

22
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:17,000
This is Modern Marvel's Top Toys and Games.

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:34,180
Hasbro was founded in 1923 with three brothers with the last name of Hassenfeld, hence Hasbro.

24
00:01:34,181 --> 00:01:40,240
In the beginning, their fledgling Rhode Island company made ends meet by selling textile remnants,

25
00:01:40,241 --> 00:01:42,138
pencil cases, school supplies.

26
00:01:42,139 --> 00:01:45,680
Eventually, they branched out to include paint, crayons,

27
00:01:45,681 --> 00:01:49,422
even toy doctor's kits for little boys and girls.

28
00:01:49,423 --> 00:01:52,400
Today, Hasbro is one of the largest toy

29
00:01:52,401 --> 00:01:57,680
manufacturers in the world with annual revenue in the neighborhood of five

30
00:01:57,681 --> 00:02:04,040
billion dollars and one of their all-time best-selling items the classic beloved

31
00:02:04,041 --> 00:02:11,280
board game Monopoly it's as popular as it ever was it's available in 114

32
00:02:11,281 --> 00:02:17,280
countries and it's been translated into 47 languages to keep up with that massive

33
00:02:17,281 --> 00:02:22,820
demand, Hasbro partnered with Cardamundi, an established card and board game company

34
00:02:22,821 --> 00:02:25,520
with roots going back to 1765.

35
00:02:25,521 --> 00:02:31,280
Today, Cardamundi is one of the largest board game manufacturers

36
00:02:31,281 --> 00:02:32,743
in the entire country.

37
00:02:32,744 --> 00:02:37,000
We're going to tour their 1.2 million square foot facility where

38
00:02:37,001 --> 00:02:42,376
400 workers create 2 million Monopoly games a year and so much more.

39
00:02:42,377 --> 00:02:43,720
I'm going to meet

40
00:02:43,721 --> 00:02:49,960
plant manager Gary Brennan to find out what has kept Hasbro and Cardamundi winning all these years

41
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:55,200
and hopefully pass go and collect my $200. I call dibs on the race car.

42
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,140
Just walking through here, the variety of how many games that are in this very building that

43
00:03:04,141 --> 00:03:05,206
blows my mind.

44
00:03:05,207 --> 00:03:11,220
We make over 200 items, but we're usually focusing on about 20 at any one time.

45
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:16,820
Items like Monopoly, Scrabble, Yahtzee, Sari, Trouble.

46
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:18,560
Items that we all grew up with.

47
00:03:18,561 --> 00:03:20,460
I don't think there's any household in America

48
00:03:20,461 --> 00:03:22,460
that isn't familiar with some of the products

49
00:03:22,461 --> 00:03:23,660
that run through here.

50
00:03:23,661 --> 00:03:25,460
What is the number one seller?

51
00:03:25,461 --> 00:03:27,540
Monopoly and or Connect Four.

52
00:03:27,541 --> 00:03:28,980
It's a race every year.

53
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:30,040
No kidding.

54
00:03:30,041 --> 00:03:32,380
And how many games roll off the line here

55
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:34,180
at Cardamundi every day?

56
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:37,220
At peak period, we'll make a million games a week.

57
00:03:37,221 --> 00:03:38,580
And it all starts right here?

58
00:03:38,581 --> 00:03:39,580
Yes, it does.

59
00:03:39,581 --> 00:03:41,220
Will you take me through the making of Monopoly?

60
00:03:41,221 --> 00:03:44,220
It would be my pleasure.

61
00:03:44,221 --> 00:03:46,860
Everyone knows this game is all about making money,

62
00:03:46,861 --> 00:03:50,360
and these bills get shuffled, traded, held,

63
00:03:50,361 --> 00:03:52,500
and spent for hours.

64
00:03:52,501 --> 00:03:55,660
So they have to be made of the best quality paper

65
00:03:55,661 --> 00:03:59,200
and printed with a very detailed printing process

66
00:03:59,201 --> 00:04:02,000
in order to withstand years of wear and tear.

67
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:03,780
I'm meeting with Jim Hare,

68
00:04:03,781 --> 00:04:06,380
the technical manager of printing at Cardamundi,

69
00:04:06,381 --> 00:04:08,940
who is going to show me how they print

70
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:13,180
half a billion dollars worth of Monopoly money every week.

71
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:17,180
Jim, how are you, sir?

72
00:04:17,181 --> 00:04:18,080
How are you?

73
00:04:18,081 --> 00:04:18,980
Good to be here.

74
00:04:18,981 --> 00:04:19,880
I'm excellent.

75
00:04:19,881 --> 00:04:20,780
So tell me what's going on here.

76
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:21,780
So this is the unit.

77
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:23,980
This is where actually the ink goes into the press.

78
00:04:23,981 --> 00:04:27,100
Up here, ink fountain holds a reservoir of ink.

79
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:29,020
Ink ball, see it spinning around.

80
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:31,420
A ductor's coming up, taking ink off,

81
00:04:31,421 --> 00:04:33,300
going into a series of rollers.

82
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:36,060
On the front, the white-looking thing is the plate.

83
00:04:36,061 --> 00:04:37,800
That has an image burned into it.

84
00:04:37,801 --> 00:04:39,720
Where the image is, we'll take the ink.

85
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:41,840
Where there's no image, it takes the water.

86
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:45,520
Jim explains to me that each aluminum printing plate they use

87
00:04:45,521 --> 00:04:48,140
has an etched image of Monopoly money.

88
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,100
When they run the plate through the machine,

89
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,200
water is attracted to all the blank areas.

90
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,440
Ink is attracted only to the etched surface.

91
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,200
So when the plate rolls onto the paper,

92
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:03,700
only the etched images of Monopoly bills are printed.

93
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:06,660
This is a cutting unit, and you can hear it.

94
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:09,800
That's the sound of the blade cutting?

95
00:05:09,801 --> 00:05:12,800
Every 28 inches, it is cutting the sheet.

96
00:05:12,801 --> 00:05:14,800
When the money is being fed that fast,

97
00:05:14,801 --> 00:05:16,800
how do you know that it's being cut precisely

98
00:05:16,801 --> 00:05:18,800
and not cutting in the middle of one of the bills?

99
00:05:18,801 --> 00:05:20,800
So we have an inspection area here.

100
00:05:20,801 --> 00:05:23,800
To get a sheet up, we'll hit this button right here.

101
00:05:23,801 --> 00:05:24,800
Okay.

102
00:05:24,801 --> 00:05:26,800
As that comes up, we can take that sheet.

103
00:05:26,801 --> 00:05:27,800
Go ahead, grab it.

104
00:05:27,801 --> 00:05:28,800
Yep.

105
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:29,800
Bring it over to the table.

106
00:05:29,801 --> 00:05:32,800
These cut marks make sure that all the Monopoly money

107
00:05:32,801 --> 00:05:34,800
is the exact right size,

108
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:38,400
so that when you hold all that Monopoly dough in your hand,

109
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:40,200
it all matches perfectly.

110
00:05:40,201 --> 00:05:43,040
And when you're printing half a billion dollars a week

111
00:05:43,041 --> 00:05:46,920
in Monopoly money, you can't afford to make mistakes.

112
00:05:46,921 --> 00:05:50,920
Next, Tim Stowers is gonna show me how they cut these sheets

113
00:05:50,921 --> 00:05:53,480
of Monopoly money into bills.

114
00:05:53,481 --> 00:05:55,520
How are you, sir? Pleasure to meet you.

115
00:05:55,521 --> 00:05:56,680
Pleasure to meet you, too.

116
00:05:56,681 --> 00:06:00,520
Tim says he cuts 18,000 to 19,000 bundles

117
00:06:00,521 --> 00:06:02,200
of Monopoly money a day,

118
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:05,540
a day, but he's not doing all of those one at a time.

119
00:06:05,541 --> 00:06:07,340
All right, will you show me how that's done?

120
00:06:07,341 --> 00:06:09,340
All right, we bring it right over here.

121
00:06:09,341 --> 00:06:10,840
Wait a minute, wait a minute.

122
00:06:10,841 --> 00:06:13,140
How the hell did you move that stack with two fingers?

123
00:06:13,141 --> 00:06:14,940
Gee, it must be that air table, huh?

124
00:06:14,941 --> 00:06:15,940
Air table?

125
00:06:15,941 --> 00:06:16,641
Yes.

126
00:06:16,642 --> 00:06:17,540
Wait a minute.

127
00:06:17,541 --> 00:06:18,640
It's got balls on it.

128
00:06:18,641 --> 00:06:21,360
Yes, balls with pressure underneath them.

129
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:24,060
It's like in the old days, if you ever played air hockey.

130
00:06:24,061 --> 00:06:24,761
Air hockey.

131
00:06:24,762 --> 00:06:26,160
Same thing.

132
00:06:26,161 --> 00:06:28,260
Similar to the way an air hockey table

133
00:06:28,261 --> 00:06:30,560
makes the puck glide above the surface,

134
00:06:30,561 --> 00:06:34,700
This table is covered with hundreds of rubber balls.

135
00:06:34,701 --> 00:06:36,560
When weight presses down on them,

136
00:06:36,561 --> 00:06:38,760
it releases tiny jets of air

137
00:06:38,761 --> 00:06:42,240
that makes moving heavy stacks of paper effortless.

138
00:06:42,241 --> 00:06:44,000
Can I try? Sure.

139
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,140
It's crazy. Look at that.

140
00:06:50,141 --> 00:06:53,720
It's a little bit like air hockey.

141
00:06:53,721 --> 00:06:55,880
That is insane.

142
00:06:55,881 --> 00:06:59,620
Sometimes it's the simple things that can be the most fun.

143
00:06:59,621 --> 00:07:05,620
In fact, some of the most memorable toys had the most simple origins,

144
00:07:05,621 --> 00:07:09,120
like in the case of one Potato Head.

145
00:07:09,121 --> 00:07:17,816
In 1952, Hasbro revolutionized the toy industry with that very special spud, Mr.

146
00:07:17,817 --> 00:07:19,120
Potato Head.

147
00:07:19,121 --> 00:07:25,120
It has sold more than 100 million units in over 30 countries.

148
00:07:25,121 --> 00:07:29,120
The toy was the brainchild of the inventor, George Lerner.

149
00:07:29,121 --> 00:07:40,840
In 1949, Lerner created toys by taking small plastic body parts with prongs and sticking them into actual potatoes and other vegetables.

150
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:47,420
The first kids came with a styrofoam head, but suggested kids use their own potato torso.

151
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:54,200
Potato Head toys flew off the shelves, raking in $4 million in the first few months.

152
00:07:54,201 --> 00:07:58,900
In the year after its debut, Mrs. Potato Head was introduced,

153
00:07:58,901 --> 00:08:01,500
and a whole potato family would follow,

154
00:08:01,501 --> 00:08:04,840
with brothers Spud and sister Yam.

155
00:08:04,841 --> 00:08:08,720
All right, so we slide these things on down effortlessly

156
00:08:08,721 --> 00:08:10,080
with the air table.

157
00:08:10,081 --> 00:08:11,520
They go into the cutter.

158
00:08:11,521 --> 00:08:12,920
What's the next step?

159
00:08:12,921 --> 00:08:14,860
Okay, make sure it's square.

160
00:08:14,861 --> 00:08:19,260
We got about 1,000 isobars of pressure.

161
00:08:19,261 --> 00:08:23,600
One isobar represents the weight of the atmosphere,

162
00:08:23,601 --> 00:08:26,600
or the weight of gravity a person would feel

163
00:08:26,601 --> 00:08:28,600
standing on the surface of the Earth.

164
00:08:28,601 --> 00:08:31,600
Multiply that pressure by 1,000,

165
00:08:31,601 --> 00:08:34,600
and you'd have the amount of force that this blade is using

166
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:37,600
to cut these stacks of money.

167
00:08:37,601 --> 00:08:42,600
That stack is solid paper, about 6 1⁄2 inches thick,

168
00:08:42,601 --> 00:08:45,600
and it's going through it like a hot knife through butter.

169
00:08:45,601 --> 00:08:48,600
So it's moving forward for the length of each build.

170
00:08:48,601 --> 00:08:50,600
Correct.

171
00:08:50,601 --> 00:08:52,600
Does it give you a warning?

172
00:08:52,601 --> 00:08:54,360
Like, is there a noise or something?

173
00:08:54,361 --> 00:08:55,360
That's what I go by.

174
00:08:55,361 --> 00:08:56,440
I go by sound.

175
00:08:56,441 --> 00:09:00,640
When Tim is done, each one of these thick stacks of paper

176
00:09:00,641 --> 00:09:05,320
will become $1.3 million worth of Monopoly money.

177
00:09:05,321 --> 00:09:09,480
That's enough money to fill almost 65 games.

178
00:09:09,481 --> 00:09:10,720
Tim, thank you for your time.

179
00:09:10,721 --> 00:09:11,421
Yes.

180
00:09:11,422 --> 00:09:13,620
On where we go.

181
00:09:13,621 --> 00:09:16,700
When people talk about their favorite toy cars,

182
00:09:16,701 --> 00:09:21,960
there is one brand that is always at the top of that list.

183
00:09:21,961 --> 00:09:24,620
With over 20,000 different variations

184
00:09:24,621 --> 00:09:27,100
and more than six billion produced,

185
00:09:27,101 --> 00:09:31,060
one of the top toys of all time are die-cast cars

186
00:09:31,061 --> 00:09:33,340
that look and race like the real thing.

187
00:09:33,341 --> 00:09:39,080
I'm talking about Hot Wheels, invented by Mattel in 1968.

188
00:09:39,081 --> 00:09:41,520
And to see how they've maintained their top toy status

189
00:09:41,521 --> 00:09:45,220
for 53 years, we're getting an inside look

190
00:09:45,221 --> 00:09:48,020
to see how they're designed at Mattel.

191
00:09:51,960 --> 00:10:02,960
I'm the design director for Hot Wheels Diecast Vehicles, and we're here at the Mattel Design

192
00:10:02,961 --> 00:10:03,960
Center.

193
00:10:03,961 --> 00:10:04,960
This is where the magic happens.

194
00:10:04,961 --> 00:10:09,960
These are toys that are first and foremost designed to appeal to kids, but at the same

195
00:10:09,961 --> 00:10:14,960
time, it's got to appeal to adults as well, because we have a huge, huge adult fan base.

196
00:10:14,961 --> 00:10:19,280
And one way we're able to do that is because many of us, myself included, actually come

197
00:10:19,281 --> 00:10:20,280
from the auto industry.

198
00:10:20,281 --> 00:10:22,180
So we're not just toy designers, you know,

199
00:10:22,181 --> 00:10:23,520
coming up with fun toys.

200
00:10:23,521 --> 00:10:26,560
We're also car designers creating really cool aesthetics

201
00:10:26,561 --> 00:10:30,220
that we know are going to appeal to both the kid and the adult.

202
00:10:30,221 --> 00:10:34,020
For a Hot Wheels basic car, it takes basically 18 months

203
00:10:34,021 --> 00:10:35,100
from start to finish.

204
00:10:35,101 --> 00:10:36,240
It's a lot of fun.

205
00:10:36,241 --> 00:10:41,200
Mattel designs over 130 brand new cars every year.

206
00:10:41,201 --> 00:10:44,900
And that process starts as a 2D concept or sketch

207
00:10:44,901 --> 00:10:46,280
from a designer.

208
00:10:46,281 --> 00:10:48,800
In the past, someone would carve the concept

209
00:10:48,801 --> 00:10:53,440
into a clay prototype, but today it goes from 2D

210
00:10:53,441 --> 00:10:55,580
to a digital 3D model.

211
00:10:55,581 --> 00:10:57,880
But to give the designers something they could touch

212
00:10:57,881 --> 00:11:02,840
and feel and inspect, this 3D image becomes a tangible object

213
00:11:02,841 --> 00:11:05,300
in the Mattel 3D printing lab.

214
00:11:05,301 --> 00:11:07,340
Right now, our PolyJet 3D printer

215
00:11:07,341 --> 00:11:10,440
is printing various Hot Wheels cars in different colors.

216
00:11:10,441 --> 00:11:13,360
It uses quite a few different resin materials

217
00:11:13,361 --> 00:11:17,520
instead of just inks to give us the highest quality prototype.

218
00:11:17,521 --> 00:11:20,120
Here we just scrape our polyjet part off.

219
00:11:20,121 --> 00:11:22,920
When the prototypes come off the 3D printer,

220
00:11:22,921 --> 00:11:25,680
they have to be cleaned from all the excess resin

221
00:11:25,681 --> 00:11:27,760
so that the designers can inspect them

222
00:11:27,761 --> 00:11:32,520
and make sure that the cars look just the way they planned.

223
00:11:32,521 --> 00:11:35,280
When the founder of Mattel invented Hot Wheels,

224
00:11:35,281 --> 00:11:38,060
he hired a rocket scientist to make sure

225
00:11:38,061 --> 00:11:42,400
that these cars could race and perform the way a car should.

226
00:11:42,401 --> 00:11:44,240
This performance is one of the reasons

227
00:11:44,241 --> 00:11:46,560
why Hot Wheels are still so popular.

228
00:11:46,561 --> 00:11:49,540
So each model has to be able to do the things

229
00:11:49,541 --> 00:11:52,120
that a kid wants them to do on the track,

230
00:11:52,121 --> 00:11:57,320
like the loop, the corner booster, and the power launcher.

231
00:11:57,321 --> 00:11:59,580
And then the last thing is the gravity drops.

232
00:11:59,581 --> 00:12:02,400
This is a test that we have a lot of fun with internally.

233
00:12:02,401 --> 00:12:04,080
We do our own internal competition.

234
00:12:04,081 --> 00:12:06,480
It needs to be able to roll at least 25 feet.

235
00:12:06,481 --> 00:12:08,400
If it doesn't, it doesn't pass, and we'll

236
00:12:08,401 --> 00:12:10,480
go back to the drawing board and make sure that we get

237
00:12:10,481 --> 00:12:11,440
everything just right.

238
00:12:11,441 --> 00:12:13,740
All right, three, two, one, go.

239
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:19,220
Oh, yeah, that's good.

240
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:30,240
One reason why Hot Wheels still attracts as many adults as they do kids is because of their Garage of Legends,

241
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:36,920
which are cars that were so popular as toys, they made them into actual cars.

242
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:40,380
All right, well, here we are. This is the Hot Wheels Garage of Legends.

243
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:44,020
As you can see around me, a bunch of really cool life-size cars.

244
00:12:44,021 --> 00:12:50,260
So the first Hot Wheels car ever produced was actually this Camaro right here.

245
00:12:50,261 --> 00:12:53,100
This is the 1967 Camaro.

246
00:12:53,101 --> 00:12:57,300
So we got this one for the kid, and of course, the big one here for mom and dad,

247
00:12:57,301 --> 00:12:58,900
the Hot Wheels fan that never grew up.

248
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:05,420
Hot Wheels aren't the only top toy that you can hold in your hand.

249
00:13:05,421 --> 00:13:11,700
One of the top toys of all time comes in the form of a puzzle cube with

250
00:13:11,701 --> 00:13:16,700
more than 43 quintillion possible combinations.

251
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:21,500
Rubik's Cube was developed in 1974

252
00:13:21,501 --> 00:13:24,260
by a guy in Hungary named Erno Rubik.

253
00:13:24,261 --> 00:13:26,400
He was a professor looking for a way

254
00:13:26,401 --> 00:13:28,040
to teach spatial relationships

255
00:13:28,041 --> 00:13:30,480
and spatial modeling to his students.

256
00:13:30,481 --> 00:13:34,660
In the US, the name was changed to Rubik's Cube,

257
00:13:34,661 --> 00:13:36,940
and it was an instant hit.

258
00:13:36,941 --> 00:13:39,660
Within the first three years after its release here,

259
00:13:39,661 --> 00:13:44,200
It sold 100 million units around the world.

260
00:13:44,201 --> 00:13:47,000
And if doing it with your hands wasn't difficult enough,

261
00:13:47,001 --> 00:13:49,880
there was actually a guy by the name of Jakub Kipa

262
00:13:49,881 --> 00:13:53,960
from Poland who solved it with his feet.

263
00:13:53,961 --> 00:13:57,280
There are also some people who can solve a Rubik's Cube

264
00:13:57,281 --> 00:14:01,440
blindfolded within 17 seconds.

265
00:14:01,441 --> 00:14:03,900
The Rubik's Cube remains popular today,

266
00:14:03,901 --> 00:14:06,900
with speed cubing tournaments being held around the world

267
00:14:06,901 --> 00:14:10,900
and people being able to solve the cube in under four seconds.

268
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:19,380
Our top toys and games are ones that we love at first sight

269
00:14:19,381 --> 00:14:22,620
and have an iconic look that we've come to love

270
00:14:22,621 --> 00:14:24,260
over years of play.

271
00:14:24,261 --> 00:14:29,260
Here at Carta Mundi, they make almost 44 million games a year.

272
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:31,700
When you see one of these games at the store

273
00:14:31,701 --> 00:14:34,660
or in your closet, it's the iconic look

274
00:14:34,661 --> 00:14:36,460
that makes you want to play.

275
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:41,000
I'm going to see how this world-famous game gets that signature look

276
00:14:41,001 --> 00:14:45,020
that makes you want to pass go and collect your $200.

277
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:48,120
How many games does this represent?

278
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:50,140
This is going to be about 10,000 games.

279
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,960
10,000 games.

280
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:56,360
And how long will it take the factory to produce 10,000 games?

281
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:59,480
We'll run these off at better than 2,000 an hour.

282
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:00,960
Oh, my gosh.

283
00:15:01,240 --> 00:15:02,660
Monopoly is a global brand.

284
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:05,500
It's 147 countries, 14 different languages.

285
00:15:05,501 --> 00:15:09,040
It is critical to the customer that the colors that are seen

286
00:15:09,041 --> 00:15:11,540
in the United States match up with colors

287
00:15:11,541 --> 00:15:13,540
that might be printed all throughout the world.

288
00:15:13,541 --> 00:15:15,840
Jim, the printing manager,

289
00:15:15,841 --> 00:15:17,540
is going to walk me through the process

290
00:15:17,541 --> 00:15:21,340
of getting all these colors just right.

291
00:15:21,341 --> 00:15:24,380
Oh, my God. That's terrifying.

292
00:15:24,381 --> 00:15:27,460
So this massive thing is a giant printer?

293
00:15:27,461 --> 00:15:28,460
All of this?

294
00:15:28,461 --> 00:15:32,460
Correct. All 125 feet of it.

295
00:15:32,461 --> 00:15:35,000
This looks like if a tractor and a printer

296
00:15:35,001 --> 00:15:36,000
had a baby.

297
00:15:36,001 --> 00:15:39,000
It's something that's pretty intimidating to stand near.

298
00:15:39,001 --> 00:15:40,500
This looks like it would kill you.

299
00:15:40,501 --> 00:15:43,500
Tell me, first of all, how many sheets get loaded,

300
00:15:43,501 --> 00:15:47,500
on average, to the typical run, or does it depend?

301
00:15:47,501 --> 00:15:50,500
This type of material, we normally run 80,000 a ship.

302
00:15:50,501 --> 00:15:52,000
That is amazing.

303
00:15:52,001 --> 00:15:53,500
Let me take it through the press.

304
00:15:53,501 --> 00:15:54,500
Please.

305
00:15:54,501 --> 00:15:56,000
Your standard desktop printer at home

306
00:15:56,001 --> 00:15:58,500
prints all four colors at the same time.

307
00:15:58,501 --> 00:16:01,000
But Jim explains that here, each sheet

308
00:16:01,001 --> 00:16:04,500
goes through a separate printer unit for each color.

309
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,840
The first pass prints the black ink.

310
00:16:07,841 --> 00:16:11,200
Then they print cyan or blue right on top of it.

311
00:16:11,201 --> 00:16:13,840
Then magenta or pink on top of that.

312
00:16:13,841 --> 00:16:14,880
Then yellow.

313
00:16:14,881 --> 00:16:21,320
And finally, the unique Pantone 345 Monopoly Green.

314
00:16:21,321 --> 00:16:22,960
So we combined all that.

315
00:16:22,961 --> 00:16:26,080
This is what we get for our end result.

316
00:16:26,081 --> 00:16:27,760
Look how beautiful that is.

317
00:16:27,761 --> 00:16:29,920
What we have is crosshairs.

318
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:32,460
Here's a magnifying glass called the loop.

319
00:16:32,461 --> 00:16:36,160
We put all that crosshair exactly on top of each other.

320
00:16:36,161 --> 00:16:38,700
So you got all five colors right there.

321
00:16:38,701 --> 00:16:42,540
When it looks like one black, you're doing good.

322
00:16:42,541 --> 00:16:44,880
But all five are right on top of each other.

323
00:16:44,881 --> 00:16:47,240
Each time a color is printed onto the page,

324
00:16:47,241 --> 00:16:49,440
it prints a tiny crosshair.

325
00:16:49,441 --> 00:16:52,080
But if all the ink levels are properly aligned,

326
00:16:52,081 --> 00:16:57,160
the crosshair will look black and have clear, straight edges.

327
00:16:57,161 --> 00:17:00,560
If any of the colors are off, the crosshairs will look blurry

328
00:17:00,561 --> 00:17:02,900
or tinted with a certain color.

329
00:17:02,901 --> 00:17:05,360
So you want it to look like black and clean.

330
00:17:05,361 --> 00:17:06,360
Correct.

331
00:17:06,361 --> 00:17:07,860
So we went through the top press.

332
00:17:07,861 --> 00:17:09,860
The middle picture is what we're printing.

333
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:11,080
That's a live camera.

334
00:17:11,081 --> 00:17:14,040
It's taking shots as the sheets are going through.

335
00:17:14,041 --> 00:17:14,880
Wait a minute.

336
00:17:14,881 --> 00:17:16,140
That's not a stock image?

337
00:17:16,141 --> 00:17:17,040
No.

338
00:17:17,041 --> 00:17:18,040
That's actually something.

339
00:17:18,041 --> 00:17:19,040
That's actually moving.

340
00:17:19,041 --> 00:17:20,740
So it's like an ultra high speed camera.

341
00:17:20,741 --> 00:17:21,740
Exactly.

342
00:17:21,741 --> 00:17:23,880
Can you show me how this beauty works?

343
00:17:23,881 --> 00:17:26,080
Right this way.

344
00:17:26,081 --> 00:17:28,260
What we have up here is the camera shooting

345
00:17:28,261 --> 00:17:30,260
through the catwalk in the floor.

346
00:17:30,261 --> 00:17:32,660
That's actually making a picture of every sheet

347
00:17:32,661 --> 00:17:34,160
as it's running by.

348
00:17:34,161 --> 00:17:38,240
That camera is shooting through a slit that's barely

349
00:17:38,241 --> 00:17:41,240
an inch and a half wide and getting a full image.

350
00:17:41,241 --> 00:17:42,080
Absolutely.

351
00:17:42,081 --> 00:17:43,280
At that rate.

352
00:17:43,281 --> 00:17:44,240
At that rate.

353
00:17:44,241 --> 00:17:48,080
Now something tells me that a printer cartridge on something

354
00:17:48,081 --> 00:17:51,740
like these is not like my Hewlett Packard at home.

355
00:17:51,741 --> 00:17:53,880
We have eight-pound cartridges of ink.

356
00:17:53,881 --> 00:17:54,880
Oh, my god.

357
00:17:54,881 --> 00:17:57,820
It's going back and forth, scanning the ink fountain.

358
00:17:57,821 --> 00:17:58,680
Oh, look, look, look.

359
00:17:58,681 --> 00:17:59,860
Yep, there it goes.

360
00:17:59,861 --> 00:18:03,360
A big bloop about the size of like a Hershey's Kiss

361
00:18:03,361 --> 00:18:05,740
that's dropped right onto the roller.

362
00:18:05,741 --> 00:18:09,780
This looks like the coolest queso fountain ever.

363
00:18:09,781 --> 00:18:13,620
It's just like if NASA made a printer, this would be it.

364
00:18:13,621 --> 00:18:15,240
I'm going to let you get back to it.

365
00:18:15,241 --> 00:18:17,700
Let's go check out the rest of the process.

366
00:18:17,701 --> 00:18:20,540
Games like Monopoly are top toys

367
00:18:20,541 --> 00:18:22,680
because they inspire the imagination

368
00:18:22,681 --> 00:18:24,680
of the would-be entrepreneur.

369
00:18:24,681 --> 00:18:28,260
But other top toys open worlds of adventure.

370
00:18:28,261 --> 00:18:31,760
Only G.I. Joe is G.I. Joe.

371
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:35,000
Possibly in response to Mattel's Barbie,

372
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:38,740
Hasbro introduced G.I. Joe in 1964

373
00:18:38,741 --> 00:18:41,320
as the world's first action figure,

374
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:43,400
and it became an instant hit.

375
00:18:43,820 --> 00:18:45,960
Within two years, G.I. Joe figures

376
00:18:45,961 --> 00:18:49,220
made up 66% of Hasbro's profits.

377
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:53,880
The first figures were 12 inches tall,

378
00:18:53,881 --> 00:18:56,080
and reportedly, Hasbro's sales team

379
00:18:56,081 --> 00:18:59,320
was prohibited from using the term doll,

380
00:18:59,321 --> 00:19:02,080
worrying that boys wouldn't want to play with it.

381
00:19:02,081 --> 00:19:05,760
So they coined the term action figure.

382
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:09,300
And G.I. Joe blazed a trail

383
00:19:09,301 --> 00:19:12,200
for generations of action figures to follow.

384
00:19:12,201 --> 00:19:16,760
But ultimately in 1978, G.I. Joe was taken off the market.

385
00:19:16,761 --> 00:19:20,940
Then in the early 80s, with the success of similar toys,

386
00:19:20,941 --> 00:19:23,340
like the Kenner Star Wars figures,

387
00:19:23,341 --> 00:19:26,440
a smaller version of G.I. Joe was brought back.

388
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:29,580
In the first year back,

389
00:19:29,581 --> 00:19:34,580
the new G.I. Joe made more than $50 million.

390
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:38,820
G.I. Joe has remained in the zeitgeist ever since.

391
00:19:41,020 --> 00:19:46,020
The action figure toy market is a $1.6 billion industry.

392
00:19:47,440 --> 00:19:50,100
And with G.I. Joe blazing the trail,

393
00:19:50,101 --> 00:19:52,500
action figures have been one of the top selling toys

394
00:19:52,501 --> 00:19:53,500
for decades.

395
00:19:55,640 --> 00:20:00,140
And in 1982, here at Mattel, they created a classic series

396
00:20:00,141 --> 00:20:05,340
that sold over $2 billion worth of action figures,

397
00:20:05,341 --> 00:20:09,820
He-Man and the Masters of the Universe.

398
00:20:09,821 --> 00:20:13,520
This series, that some say was based on Conan the Barbarian,

399
00:20:13,521 --> 00:20:19,020
lorded over 20% of the action figure market in the early 80s.

400
00:20:19,021 --> 00:20:21,020
At the time, there was nothing else

401
00:20:21,021 --> 00:20:22,920
like Masters on the market.

402
00:20:22,921 --> 00:20:26,560
These were figures that were of a totally different size

403
00:20:26,561 --> 00:20:28,520
and scale than kids were used to.

404
00:20:28,521 --> 00:20:32,460
They were these beefy, chunky, five and a half inch characters

405
00:20:32,461 --> 00:20:34,800
that were muscular, they were heroic.

406
00:20:34,801 --> 00:20:37,440
This was at a time when the biggest players in the game

407
00:20:37,441 --> 00:20:39,940
would have been Hasbro with their GI Joe line,

408
00:20:39,941 --> 00:20:41,780
and of course, Kenner with Star Wars.

409
00:20:41,781 --> 00:20:44,220
And those were 3.75 inch figures

410
00:20:44,221 --> 00:20:46,620
that had a much thinner frame.

411
00:20:46,621 --> 00:20:48,580
But there was something extra special

412
00:20:48,581 --> 00:20:51,020
about He-Man and the Masters of the universe.

413
00:20:51,021 --> 00:20:53,780
that made it so powerful for kids,

414
00:20:53,781 --> 00:20:56,660
and kids were part of how it all began.

415
00:20:56,661 --> 00:21:00,320
Kids actually drove the I have the power,

416
00:21:00,321 --> 00:21:03,400
classic He-Man statement when he transforms.

417
00:21:03,401 --> 00:21:06,400
Some years ago in a testing session

418
00:21:06,401 --> 00:21:08,040
in a consumer panel with kids,

419
00:21:08,041 --> 00:21:09,600
kids kept talking about power.

420
00:21:09,601 --> 00:21:11,300
They kept talking about the weaponry,

421
00:21:11,301 --> 00:21:15,280
and they helped create the expression I have the power,

422
00:21:15,281 --> 00:21:18,020
which has now transcended Masters of the Universe

423
00:21:18,021 --> 00:21:21,280
It has become a pop culture iconic statement.

424
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:24,720
They had five years of glory.

425
00:21:24,721 --> 00:21:29,140
82 to 87 was just massive.

426
00:21:29,141 --> 00:21:32,020
They ended up selling $400 million worth

427
00:21:32,021 --> 00:21:35,640
of Masters of the Universe toys in 1986,

428
00:21:35,641 --> 00:21:38,720
and then selling 7 million in 1987.

429
00:21:38,721 --> 00:21:41,780
It crashed spectacularly.

430
00:21:41,781 --> 00:21:44,920
But true top toys never die.

431
00:21:44,921 --> 00:21:50,760
Starting in 2021, He-Man is back in a huge, muscular way

432
00:21:50,761 --> 00:21:54,520
with a new Netflix series and an all new line of action

433
00:21:54,521 --> 00:21:56,760
figures designed right here at Mattel.

434
00:21:56,761 --> 00:22:00,480
And we get to see how they designed this top toy.

435
00:22:04,040 --> 00:22:06,520
I designed the action figure and see it through the process

436
00:22:06,521 --> 00:22:09,000
from concept into a final production.

437
00:22:09,001 --> 00:22:10,560
Back in the day, the vintage figure

438
00:22:10,561 --> 00:22:12,480
only had six points of articulation.

439
00:22:12,481 --> 00:22:14,320
And what we're doing now is modernizing it.

440
00:22:14,321 --> 00:22:18,160
with being a bigger scale, more detail, more articulation,

441
00:22:18,161 --> 00:22:19,700
about 30 points of articulation.

442
00:22:19,701 --> 00:22:22,460
First, we start by getting the animation reference

443
00:22:22,461 --> 00:22:24,640
from the Netflix animation.

444
00:22:24,641 --> 00:22:27,400
We start off with a drawing, in this case, He-Man.

445
00:22:27,401 --> 00:22:31,360
We need to translate this 2D art into a 3D action figure.

446
00:22:31,361 --> 00:22:34,360
And then we have to make a sheet outlining

447
00:22:34,361 --> 00:22:38,560
what comes with the figure, what are the specs, the sizes,

448
00:22:38,561 --> 00:22:40,780
and what we want as far as articulation.

449
00:22:40,781 --> 00:22:43,880
Next, it goes to the 3D animation team.

450
00:22:43,881 --> 00:22:46,800
Here in the sculpting department, we get the designs from our designer,

451
00:22:46,801 --> 00:22:50,100
and we bring it in the computer, and we just start using the reference that they give us.

452
00:22:50,101 --> 00:22:53,840
And we use this special device called GeoMagic Touch.

453
00:22:53,841 --> 00:22:58,400
And using this, it's just like a pen, but in 3D, we can just move it around.

454
00:22:58,401 --> 00:23:01,440
It's just like sculpting with hand, but we just do it on the computer instead.

455
00:23:01,441 --> 00:23:04,980
It's sort of like a really high-tech mouse stylus.

456
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:08,480
And when I'm touching the piece in the computer, I can actually feel it.

457
00:23:08,481 --> 00:23:12,620
It gives me force feedback, sort of like your game controller when you get shot.

458
00:23:12,621 --> 00:23:14,520
You can feel that little rumble.

459
00:23:14,521 --> 00:23:16,060
So this kind of gives me that feedback

460
00:23:16,061 --> 00:23:17,700
so I can feel it as I'm sculpting.

461
00:23:17,701 --> 00:23:20,000
So it kind of mimics that hand sculpting motion

462
00:23:20,001 --> 00:23:21,800
that you can't really get with a mouse.

463
00:23:21,801 --> 00:23:25,800
So here we have Skeletor, which is He-Man's main nemesis.

464
00:23:25,801 --> 00:23:27,140
And I can come in here with a tool

465
00:23:27,141 --> 00:23:28,560
and manipulate his face a little bit.

466
00:23:28,561 --> 00:23:29,500
Let's get him on the nose.

467
00:23:29,501 --> 00:23:31,580
There's a rough nose on Skeletor.

468
00:23:31,581 --> 00:23:33,280
He doesn't look as evil.

469
00:23:33,281 --> 00:23:35,300
Interesting, I've never seen him with a nose before.

470
00:23:35,301 --> 00:23:37,380
With this program, if designers can dream it,

471
00:23:37,381 --> 00:23:39,120
we can pretty much make it.

472
00:23:39,121 --> 00:23:41,680
After this, it's off to 3D printing.

473
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:50,460
Today we are printing a couple of Masters of the Universe figures.

474
00:23:50,461 --> 00:23:52,660
We have a He-Man and a Skeletor.

475
00:23:52,661 --> 00:24:00,040
Here at Mattel's 3D printing room, they use a stereo lithography 3D printer, which fires

476
00:24:00,041 --> 00:24:05,260
a laser into liquid resin, curing it almost instantly.

477
00:24:05,261 --> 00:24:11,860
The tray lowers itself by very small increments until each layer of the model is completed.

478
00:24:11,861 --> 00:24:15,340
Then, after a quick power wash, they hand them back

479
00:24:15,341 --> 00:24:18,980
to the design team for review.

480
00:24:18,981 --> 00:24:23,100
Here we have the final He-Man and Skeletor action figures.

481
00:24:23,101 --> 00:24:25,580
Looking at all the pieces, I can remember

482
00:24:25,581 --> 00:24:30,020
all the functionality that I specified on the figure.

483
00:24:30,021 --> 00:24:32,060
And I think it turned out pretty good.

484
00:24:32,061 --> 00:24:34,860
And I think kids are going to have fun playing with this.

485
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:38,060
And adults as well.

486
00:24:41,860 --> 00:24:46,860
There are so many top games made right here at Cardamundi.

487
00:24:46,861 --> 00:24:48,860
But I'm taking a break from Monopoly

488
00:24:48,861 --> 00:24:50,860
to see how they make the board of another game

489
00:24:50,861 --> 00:24:54,860
that families were playing even before Monopoly.

490
00:24:54,861 --> 00:24:57,860
It's a game based on the Indian crosses and circles game,

491
00:24:57,861 --> 00:25:02,860
Parcheesi. But you know it as Sorry.

492
00:25:02,861 --> 00:25:04,860
So break down the process for me.

493
00:25:04,861 --> 00:25:06,860
What happens along the line?

494
00:25:06,861 --> 00:25:11,860
The machine has a divider that separates two panels.

495
00:25:11,861 --> 00:25:14,900
The two panels get fed into the wheel.

496
00:25:14,901 --> 00:25:19,780
You have one, two, three stations that fold all four sides.

497
00:25:19,781 --> 00:25:23,440
Leo explains that the process starts with chipboards.

498
00:25:23,441 --> 00:25:25,420
They'll take some sorry game sheets,

499
00:25:25,421 --> 00:25:28,640
hot off the printer, and glue it onto the chipboard.

500
00:25:28,641 --> 00:25:31,580
Then it gets cut, scored, and folded.

501
00:25:31,581 --> 00:25:32,720
What is this?

502
00:25:32,721 --> 00:25:33,520
That's glue.

503
00:25:33,521 --> 00:25:35,280
You said this is the glue?

504
00:25:35,281 --> 00:25:36,720
Yes.

505
00:25:36,721 --> 00:25:39,680
I've got a tank on the other side that will actually melt it

506
00:25:39,681 --> 00:25:41,780
as it's needed.

507
00:25:41,781 --> 00:25:44,100
So this is how it's loaded, just like this?

508
00:25:44,101 --> 00:25:44,801
Yeah.

